# Story: someone who lives on the keyboard writes in Notes with the pointer left over Safari. The shortcuts
# mean Notes. Then they point at Safari, and the shortcuts mean Safari, until they go back to typing.
# Reference: T31, A56 (docs/features/F56-keys-follow-the-keyboard.md). Window ids start at 101.
open Safari
open Notes
open Mail
dock 101 left
dock 102 right
dock 103 beside 102 bottom
# The pointer is left over Safari; ⌘Tab to Mail, and type.
cursor 200 400
wait 5
focus 103
type
wait 0.3
# ⌃⌥↓ twice: into the group that holds Mail, then onto Mail. Safari, under the pointer, goes out of view.
press in
expect visible 102
expect visible 103
expect hidden 101
wait 0.5
press in
expect camera 103
# A pinch means what is under the pointer, whatever the keyboard is doing.
press show-all
expect camera root
from gesture in
expect camera 101
press show-all
# The focus went to Safari when Mail left the view. Back to Mail, and type.
focus 103
type
wait 2
# Pointing at Safari and pressing ⌃⌥↓ means Safari…
cursor 220 420
wait 0.4
press in
expect camera 101
# …and so do the shortcuts that follow, however long one looks first.
wait 4
press out
expect camera root
wait 4
press in
expect camera 101
# A second display is plugged in, with Terminal and Slack side by side on it and Terminal framed.
# Safari is still framed on the main display.
displays 2
open Terminal
open Slack
move 104 1600 100 500 400
move 105 2400 100 500 400
dock 104 left
dock 105 right
zoom 104
expect fills 101
expect hidden 105
# The trap: click into Terminal, push the mouse aside onto the main display, type.
cursor 1700 300
focus 104
wait 1
cursor 200 400
wait 1
type
wait 0.3
# ⌃⌥↑ zooms out the display being typed on. The one under the pointer stays as it was.
press out
expect visible 105
expect fills 101
expect hidden 102
# A pinch, and a shortcut right after moving the pointer, mean the display under the pointer.
from gesture out
expect visible 102
expect visible 103
